#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189365 :tutorial/content "The **rank-nullity theorem in terms of linear transformations** states the following:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation, then* $$\\textrm{dim(Im}(\\mathbf{T})) + \\textrm{dim(Ker}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),$$ *or equivalently,* $$\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n.$$\n\nFor example, suppose that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation such that $$\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{ [ x, y, z ]^T \\, : \\, y+2z=0\\}.$$\n\nLet's use the rank-nullity theorem to compute $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})$ and $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$\n\nFirst, solving $y+2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y = -2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as $$\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis means that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}.$ And since $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are linearly independent, we have $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=2.$\n\nFinally, using the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain $$\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\ & =3-2 \\\\ & =1.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9c4215af-a9cf-5a1a-aabc-40c5395f2df4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4379 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
